<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CoreLib\CoreLib.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Plugin.BLE" Version="3.2.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="QRCoder" Version="1.8.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <!-- WPF drives the dashboard. WinForms stays enabled only for NotifyIcon, which WPF
         has no equivalent for, and for the Win32 clipboard interop. -->
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- Our own [STAThread] Main is the entry point, so the SDK must not generate one. -->
    <StartupObject>WinDaemon.Program</StartupObject>
    <EnableDefaultApplicationDefinition>false</EnableDefaultApplicationDefinition>
    <ApplicationHighDpiMode>PerMonitorV2</ApplicationHighDpiMode>
    <!-- Shown on the executable, in the taskbar and in Alt+Tab. Without it the window
         fell back to the generic default icon. -->
    <ApplicationIcon>Resources\meshsync.ico</ApplicationIcon>

    <!--
      WHY THIS IS HERE. `PublishSingleFile` bundles managed assemblies and nothing else: the five
      native libraries WPF needs - `wpfgfx_cor3`, `PresentationNative_cor3`, `D3DCompiler_47_cor3`,
      `PenImc_cor3` and `vcruntime140_cor3` - are left in the publish directory beside the .exe.
      Every release from v0.1.0 to v0.6.0 attached only the .exe, so what people downloaded was a
      single file missing a fifth of itself.

      It fails in the worst way available. The process starts, loads the identity, opens the TCP
      listener and begins advertising over Bluetooth, and only then does the first window throw
      `DllNotFoundException` from `SetWindowLongPtrWndProc`. There is no window, no tray icon and
      no console to print to - and the process stays alive, so the second double-click is refused
      by the single-instance mutex and does nothing at all. "It doesn't open" is the whole of what
      a person sees.

      It is stated here rather than passed on the publish command line because the property is the
      correctness of the artifact, not a choice about it: a publish that turns single-file on must
      take the native libraries with it. Turning single-file on is still the caller's decision -
      this does nothing when it is off, which is how the installer's payload is built.
    -->
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Also embedded as a resource so the window and tray can load it at runtime. -->
    <Resource Include="Resources\meshsync.ico" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
